WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.980
Thank you so much for
that introduction, Hadley.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.690
It is such a
pleasure to be here.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:24.530
Max and I are really excited
to be talking to you today

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:29.190
about machine learning and
software for machine learning.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.550
This talk, the
topic is not so much

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:35.150
going to be about the math
of statistical modeling

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.440
or new methods for
machine learning.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.430
What we want to talk
to you here today about

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:47.390
is applied machine learning, how
you as a modeling practitioner

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.380
get on that road, that
road from model development

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:54.110
through to model deployment.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:58.580
As we're here together and we
talk about being this road that

00:00:58.580 --> 00:00:59.430
we are on.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.890
We think this trip
down this road

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:04.250
is going to be better
if we all go together.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.150
So we would encourage you to
have Slido up on your phone

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.820
with the event
code, Rkeynote,

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.840
and we're going to be using
some polls during this talk to,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.960
for us in this
room and people who

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.840
are watching from afar
to be able to share

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:25.760
their own experiences,
opinions and perspectives.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.130
So, what we're going to
be talking about today

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.100
is the software that we
build for machine learning,

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.250
and that is tidymodels.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:39.320
So you can think of tidymodels
as a meta package that

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.490
helps you install and
manage other packages that

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.090
are used for modeling
and machine learning in

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.950
R, these packages
work together,

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:53.330
but each have a
specific focus. 

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:56.010
It works much the same way
that the tidyverse does.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.270
So if you have ever
typed library tidyverse

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:04.760
and then used ggplot2 for
data visualization, dplyr

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.150
for data manipulation, you
can think about tidymodels

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:12.590
as working in the same way
you type library tidymodels,

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:16.130
and then you have access to
these different packages that

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.760
have a focused purpose.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.450
So yardstick, for
example, is a package

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:25.100
that is used to measure model
performance. Tune, perhaps

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.640
unsurprisingly, is a package
for hyperparameter tuning.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.370
So this is a great way to
think about tidymodels

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.860
that it's an R package
that gives you access

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.740
to all these kinds of
functions that work together.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:41.300
It's also right to be
thinking about tidymodels

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.180
as a unified
framework for modeling

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.840
in R. The ecosystem
for modeling

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.560
in R is one of
our big strengths.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:57.050
It is a reason why people choose
to use R. At the same time it

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:01.460
really presents a challenge
because the interface to all

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.110
these different kinds of
models are very heterogeneous.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:08.750
If you have set up
a model analysis

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:11.150
using one kind of
model, the moving

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.460
over to a different
type of model

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.190
often involves starting
over from scratch

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:17.870
with your whole approach.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.890
Tidymodels provides
a unified interface

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:26.870
to a vast array of modeling
approaches, feature engineering

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.920
approaches, and encourages
good statistical practice

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:32.953
via its design.

00:03:32.953 --> 00:03:35.370
Now some of you are sitting
out there and you're thinking,

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:39.120
wait a minute, R already
has several of these.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.520
In fact, that person
standing there

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.320
next to Julia
talking right now is

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.130
one of the people who
has built one already.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.760
So, Max, what's going on?

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.370
Why yet another modeling
framework for R? Why

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:54.460
would Max do this,

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:55.920
right? 

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.010
So I wrote a package a
while back called caret,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.100
and it's another similar
modeling framework.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.500
And it was in 2005,
which many of you know,

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.260
if you develop packages back
then, we're a different time.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.107
There were no namespaces
in R, for example,

00:04:09.107 --> 00:04:11.440
and it was lacking a lot of
the things that we have now.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.440
I was also kind of doing
it in my spare time,

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.270
and it wasn't really
engineered in a way

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.100
that we could sort
of port it over

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.910
to new and better interfaces
and extend it really well.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:24.270
So, you know, learning more
as I've gone on about software

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:26.940
engineering, I think if I'd
known those things back then,

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.460
I would have done some things
different under the hood.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:30.835
So it's not really
something that

00:04:30.835 --> 00:04:32.530
is really extendable
at this point.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:34.710
But maybe more
importantly, it's really

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.310
not something that has,
it's not a bad interface,

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.620
but it's a very
2005 interface where

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.080
you have one function that
does just about everything

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.210
and has like 30 options to it.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:47.820
So we were thinking about doing
more and more with caret.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.040
It would be really
difficult because if we

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.550
add more types of sampling or
more pre processors or this

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.170
or that, then you
have a function that

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.510
has like maybe 100
or so options, which

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:58.900
is not a good design.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.020
And so since 2005,
I think we've really

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.780
learned a lot more about
how to write a good R package,

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.060
but we've learned a lot more
about how people interact

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:07.590
with our software a lot more.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.090
And then, you know,
as time goes on,

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.160
you see things that are
happening in ggplot

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.440
even just plyr and then dplyr,
and things like that.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.330
So I think we've learned
like as a community,

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:19.140
a lot more about what are good
ways of writing interfaces

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.230
to packages.

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:22.215
And then as I started
to think about that,

00:05:22.215 --> 00:05:24.090
I think about this in
the context of modeling

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.010
and it seemed really
like it would make sense

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:29.500
to have more of like a tidy
modern interface to modeling.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:32.910
And so that's really the reason
to build a whole new sort

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.160
of stack from the beginning,
from the bottom up,

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:38.670
because really what was
old wasn't engineered

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:39.970
well enough to extend.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:42.810
And also just its whole,
its whole view of the world

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:44.230
was not really very modern.

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:46.620
So that's why we're going
to do something different.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.080
So a lot of times we want to
introduce software and talk

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.382
about, let's say, our
philosophy and why we do things.

00:05:51.382 --> 00:05:53.340
A lot of times we want
to start off with a data

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.130
set that we can use
to illustrate things.

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:57.370
So here's the data set that

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:59.420
some of you have seen
it's Spotify data.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:02.700
So each row in the data
frame is a track or a song.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.450
We might want to do
some modeling on this.

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:06.840
This was in a Sliced competition.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:10.830
The outcome is the popularity
column right there.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:11.675
That's a numeric.

00:06:11.675 --> 00:06:13.050
So we want to try
to predict that

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:15.360
with the other columns
that are in this data set.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.300
Other interesting features,
there's an artist feature

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.270
and as you might imagine,
there are quite a large number

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:22.883
of artists in the
Spotify catalog.

00:06:22.883 --> 00:06:25.050
So we might think about
ways we could deal with that

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.030
when we go to do modeling.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.250
There's a date field
and we might not

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.500
want to put date
directly into a model.

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:34.440
We might be better off by
deriving features from it,

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.790
like an indicator for month
or year or any seasonality

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.160
or any sort of
date-based features

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.860
instead of using the
original date value.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:45.510
And then we sort of
have this monster

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.250
of a column over
here, this character,

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:50.460
and it's really like a
multiple choice type field,

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:52.300
sort in a delimited format.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:53.855
And what we'd like
to do is we think

00:06:53.855 --> 00:06:55.980
that maybe genres are
probably important to predict

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.040
popularity.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:59.200
So we'd have to deal
with this data somehow.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.610
We'd have to find all the
genres that are in the data.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:03.510
Figure out how to encode
them into our model

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:06.270
matrix or the data we hand
off to the model in a way

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.280
that we could make that work.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.310
And so there's this
sort of idea that we

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:15.190
have of like what our modeling
script is going to look like.

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:17.670
And we read books
and we see websites

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.060
and blogs and things like that.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:21.060
I think we're always led
to believe that there's

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:25.260
this perfect data set that
were handed by our associates

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:28.418
and there's a single call
to a modeling function.

00:07:28.418 --> 00:07:30.210
It's really clean and
nice, and then you're

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.640
going to make predictions and
it's just like a single line

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:33.460
and you're fine.

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:34.540
It's no big deal.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:36.520
But the reality
is more like this.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:37.930
It's like a
Franken-script.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.830
So, you know, back when
I used to do modeling,

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:43.860
I had a lot of scripts that I
like to handle multiple choice

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:46.050
values that I'd use
across different projects

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:47.980
and then different jobs
and things like that.

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:49.440
So I think this is
more the reality

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.620
of how most of our
modeling scripts look like.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:55.117
And a lot of this is about
the data preprocessing.

00:07:55.117 --> 00:07:56.700
So, you know, at the
top there like,

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.220
we might use something like
a technique called effect

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.160
encoding to handle the
large number of artists that

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.720
require some
statistical estimation

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:06.330
of the effect of each artist.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.550
You might do things convert
your, you know, all of us

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:10.340
have lubridate commands, right?

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:12.180
That use like
wday and all that

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:15.270
to make date features that
we then put in our model.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:18.090
There's code there to parse
the multiple choice field

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:20.392
and then at some point, we
have way too many features.

00:08:20.392 --> 00:08:22.600
So we might think about
doing some feature selection.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.390
And so there's this
big scripts that we

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:27.210
have to do before we get
to that actual line, one

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:28.830
line of modeling.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:30.250
So this is the
reality of things.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:31.060
And it's not great.

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:33.059
I mean, it's not something
if you do this for a living,

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:34.892
you want to have to
deal with every time you

00:08:34.892 --> 00:08:36.500
get a new data set.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:38.358
Now we'll come
back to that slide

00:08:38.358 --> 00:08:40.650
and we'll also come back to
this slide couple of times.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:42.525
And the point we want
to make with this slide

00:08:42.525 --> 00:08:45.590
is that we're doing even
before we get to the model,

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:49.220
we're doing a lot of estimation
and a lot of quasi-modeling

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:49.742
work.

00:08:49.742 --> 00:08:51.200
So there are things
here that we're

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.330
estimating from a
statistical standpoint

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:55.520
and also to say that when
we go to predict new data,

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.370
we have to apply all those
same transformations to data

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:01.530
we might get six months from
now to make a prediction.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:03.830
So a good chunk of that,
that sort of script

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:08.510
on the last slide has to carry
over to your other prediction

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:11.150
script and you have
to keep using it.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.150
So it's a bit of a mess.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:14.130
And that's somebody who
did this for a living.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:15.713
Like this is a great
problem to solve,

00:09:15.713 --> 00:09:17.960
and I think it's
probably pretty solvable.

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:20.750
I really love this
visualization of what

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:22.740
you think your script
is going to look like.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:25.220
And how it actually
ends up turning out

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:28.260
aligns so much with my
practical experience.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.170
And that's why we
want to talk to you

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:34.670
here about why tidymodels
is designed the way it is

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:39.890
and how it can support
more ergonomic approaches

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:40.860
to modeling.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:43.160
By ergonomic, what
we mean here, this

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:46.460
is really about syntax,
about how expressive

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:50.600
you can be in the code that you
write, how fluent you are in,

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:53.420
and how, how, how the
syntax that you write

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:55.100
helps you get your work done.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:57.890
We also want to talk
about how tidymodels will

00:09:57.890 --> 00:09:59.670
make you more effective.

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:02.570
What we mean by
effective here is

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:06.980
that it allows you as
a practical modeling

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:12.230
practitioner, to have access to
a wide swath of different kinds

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:15.440
of approaches. That
it enables you to do

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:17.460
many different kinds of things.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:19.970
And last, we want to
talk to you about how

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:22.250
tidymodels keeps you safe.

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:27.530
The process of machine learning
has many pitfalls or potholes

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:29.330
in the road on the
way, if you will,

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:31.910
and what tidy, tidymodels is designed

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:36.240
to help you avoid those
pitfalls, those potholes.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.870
So, Max, let's
start with talking

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:41.820
about being more ergonomic.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:43.970
What, how do you think
how do you think

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:46.670
about being ergonomic
when it comes to modeling?

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:48.990
So a lot of times I think
about physical ergonomics.

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:50.690
Like when I worked
in diagnostics, we

00:10:50.690 --> 00:10:52.363
have people come
in with clinicians

00:10:52.363 --> 00:10:54.530
and they would interact
with the diagnostic machines

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:56.780
we had and we'd
watch them and see

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:59.175
like just as they did their
work, like what were things

00:10:59.175 --> 00:11:01.800
they did over and over again or
they might get frustrated with.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.760
And there's a lot of that in
the syntax, the way we design

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:07.970
syntax for our functions, but in
particular to machine learning,

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:10.530
there's an extra layer of things
that we have to think about,

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:13.040
because there's a high amount
of cognitive load for machine

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:13.740
learning.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:15.770
So when you go to do a
model for the first time,

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:17.545
or if you're, let's
say, taking a workshop

00:11:17.545 --> 00:11:19.670
to learn about machine
learning before you can even

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:20.790
get to your first model fit.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:22.957
There's all this stuff that
we have to throw at you.

00:11:22.957 --> 00:11:25.430
We have to talk about
overfitting in data splitting

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:27.800
and training and test sets,
and there's a lot of jargon

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:28.690
and a lot of things.

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:30.590
It's like, you do this and
then this and then this,

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:32.840
and then, you know, a day
or two later or maybe you're

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:33.920
fitting your first model.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:35.420
And so what we want
to do is we want

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:37.820
to simplify this process,
maybe in ways you don't even

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:40.280
see in terms of the syntax.

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:42.200
So we want to take
this complexity

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:43.740
and reduce it down a little bit.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:46.830
We don't want to stop you from doing
really sophisticated things.

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:49.490
And having access to the
functions and the nitty

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:50.370
gritty bits.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:52.190
But we do want to give
you really good APIs

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:54.230
that for like 80% of
the work, the syntax

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:56.930
should be pretty high level
and consumable by just

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:58.190
about anybody.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:11:59.960
So one aspect of
machine learning

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:01.820
and really complicated
or sophisticated

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:05.297
statistical models is, we have
to think about what our data are

00:12:05.297 --> 00:12:06.630
and how we're going to use them.

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:08.510
So we're going to do
our first Slido poll.

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:11.310
So go ahead to slido.com
if you haven't already.

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:14.095
And the key or the event
code is Rkeynote.

00:12:14.095 --> 00:12:15.470
And so what we
want to talk about

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:17.060
is the types of
roles you would have

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:18.900
when you're building models.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:20.310
And so this is multiple choice.

00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:22.185
So like A and B are the
things that we probably

00:12:22.185 --> 00:12:24.415
think about normally,
predictors and outcomes.

00:12:24.415 --> 00:12:26.540
But, you know, there are
more sophisticated methods

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:29.330
you might use with case
weights, like frequency weights,

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:31.670
or importance weights,
or survey weights.

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:33.290
In some cases, you
might be modeling

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:35.040
and you have
stratification variables.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:38.850
Have you ever used those? Things
like censoring indicators?

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:42.140
So Hannah Frick will be talking
about our code or our package

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:43.410
for survival analysis.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:45.930
If you've ever used that,
like choose this on the quiz.

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:47.555
And then there's some
old school things

00:12:47.555 --> 00:12:50.340
like offsets for Poisson
data and things like that.

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:52.280
So the point is
like we're normally

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:54.418
thinking about things in
a pretty simplistic way,

00:12:54.418 --> 00:12:56.210
but once we get more
and more sophisticated

00:12:56.210 --> 00:12:57.530
and the types of models
that we want to fit

00:12:57.530 --> 00:12:59.390
and the types of
problems that we have,

00:12:59.390 --> 00:13:00.780
it actually gets more complex.

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:02.780
And we want to make sure
that complexity for you

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:06.020
doesn't get overwhelming as
we work with tidy models.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:07.820
So let's look at
recipes for a minute,

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:10.480
so we'll use that as an example.

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:13.840
So what recipes are are a kind
of a combination of the R formula

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.900
method that you see
in modeling functions

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:19.840
sort of combined with like
a dplyr sort of approach

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:23.023
where you're going to be
basically piping in operations

00:13:23.023 --> 00:13:25.690
that you want to do to your data
before you get it to the model.

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:27.100
So in the first line
there, we learned

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.100
that we load the tidymodels package, which

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:31.270
again loads the
core tidymodels packages

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.230
plus some tidyverse packages.

00:13:33.230 --> 00:13:35.055
And when you start
modeling, a lot

00:13:35.055 --> 00:13:36.430
of things that we
typically do is

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:37.900
we do an initial
split of our data

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:39.410
into testing and training set.

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:41.770
So that second line is just
taking our data set that's

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:44.380
in a Spotify data frame
and then converting

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:48.220
that to an object that has
all the indicators for row 7

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:51.260
goes in training, row 10
goes in testing and so on.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:53.260
And so when you want to
access the training set,

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:55.390
if you look at the right
hand side of the third line,

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:57.223
you'll see that we can
run a function called

00:13:57.223 --> 00:13:59.140
training on that
split object and that

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:01.960
will return the data frame that
corresponds to the training

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:02.490
set.

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:04.240
So in that first line,
what we're doing is

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:07.760
we're starting a recipe and
there's a formula method there.

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:10.340
It says popularity tilde dot.

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:13.400
The left hand side says
popularity is an outcome.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:14.955
That's its role in the analysis.

00:14:14.955 --> 00:14:16.330
And the dot means
everything else

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:18.290
that's in that data
frame besides popularity

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:20.942
should be, by default, considered to be a predictor.

00:14:20.942 --> 00:14:23.150
Now you can have a lot of
different roles in recipes.

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:25.327
You can make up roles
for whatever you want.

00:14:25.327 --> 00:14:27.160
But right now, at this
point, all the recipe

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:29.270
does is it sort of
catalogs the data.

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:31.340
What's in the dataframe,
is it numeric?

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:33.040
Is it date is a
categorical that sort

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:35.380
of thing, and then it
assigns it like a basic role

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:36.760
for the analysis.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:39.400
So what we can do then is
as we want to do operations

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:41.710
on our data to prepare
it for modeling,

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:43.570
we can sort of
pipe in new steps,

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:46.870
new functions that
represent data processing

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:48.408
steps in our recipe.

00:14:48.408 --> 00:14:50.950
So let's say we're going to use
a model like a neural network

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:52.240
or nearest neighbor
model where we

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:53.948
have to make sure that
our predictors are

00:14:53.948 --> 00:14:54.950
all in the same units.

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:57.325
And one common way of doing
that is centering and scaling

00:14:57.325 --> 00:14:57.910
your data.

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:01.035
So a step function we have for
that is called step normalize.

00:15:01.035 --> 00:15:02.410
And after step
normalize, you can

00:15:02.410 --> 00:15:05.680
use any sort of dplyr selectors
like you would in the dplyr

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:08.230
select function to say which
variable should be affected

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:10.600
by this can use contains
and starts with,

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:14.000
or you can just list the
actual variables like unquoted.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.060
But we know from the
modeling standpoint,

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:17.800
when we make choices
about these things,

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.170
we're thinking about what
type of role the variable is

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:21.320
and what its type is.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:23.230
You wouldn't go and
try to center and scale

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:24.770
like categorical data.

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:27.070
So what we've done is
we've given recipes

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:29.470
a lot of extra dplyr
selectors to let

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:31.930
you choose the model
related context in which you

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:33.140
want to use variables.

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:34.930
So you see that all
nominal or I'm sorry,

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:37.207
all numeric predictors
and it's doing

00:15:37.207 --> 00:15:39.040
what you think. It's
going to select anything

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.890
at that point that exists in
the recipe that is numeric

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:43.237
and has a role of predictor.

00:15:43.237 --> 00:15:45.070
So when we think about
the complexity of how

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:46.990
we work with and
handle data, we're

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:48.520
adding a lot of
bells and whistles

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:52.270
that are like quite literal that
will make your use of recipes

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:55.930
and more complicated
functions go a lot easier.

00:15:55.930 --> 00:15:59.350
So when we go back to our what
I keep calling the Franken

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:01.570
script, you know, we
have all these things

00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:04.130
that we wanted to do our data
before we give it to the model.

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:07.130
And it's and it's kind of, like
I said, a big mess of scripts.

00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:08.440
You know, parts of these
scripts are probably

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:10.490
never been like unit tested
and things like that.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.573
It's this stuff that you've
had along for the ride

00:16:12.573 --> 00:16:16.190
for a few years, and so we can
convert that into a recipe.

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:18.490
So in that yellow box
you see a series of steps

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:21.190
and they do all the things
that we talked about doing.

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:25.840
You might use the step date
to take the date field that's

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:29.380
in there and convert that to
new features for date and year

00:16:29.380 --> 00:16:30.440
and things like that.

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:32.770
The step after that is
the step that we have that

00:16:32.770 --> 00:16:35.990
is good for parsing multiple
choice columns that are text.

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:38.680
So we get all the genres, make
all the indicator variables

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:40.520
for them, which is quite a lot.

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:42.190
But at the end of
that you might think

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:44.500
you have too many variables
to put in your model,

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:46.900
so you might want to do some
sort of unsupervised feature

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:49.010
filter to get rid of
redundant predictors.

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:51.470
You might have two genres that
kind of do the same thing.

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:52.730
They're almost identical.

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:54.400
And so the step
below that step core

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:57.070
is one that can actually
remove predictors in case

00:16:57.070 --> 00:16:58.730
their collinearity
or their their.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:00.322
pairwise correlations
are too high.

00:17:00.322 --> 00:17:02.530
So all these things you can
put into a single object,

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:05.297
you can save that object,
you can carry it around.

00:17:05.297 --> 00:17:06.589
It's not in a bunch of scripts.

00:17:06.589 --> 00:17:10.010
It's been unit tested and it
has a lot of features in it.

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:12.460
So we want to simplify
that part of the process.

00:17:12.460 --> 00:17:14.698
So the last three lines
in the yellow block,

00:17:14.698 --> 00:17:16.990
we do something like we create
what's called a workflow

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:18.970
object, and that's
a situation where

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:21.940
you want to take a complicated
preprocessor like a recipe

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:24.220
and bind it together to
whatever type of model

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:25.250
that you want to use.

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:26.829
And in this case, we're going
to use a machine learning

00:17:26.829 --> 00:17:27.859
model called cubist.

00:17:27.859 --> 00:17:31.250
It's a very sophisticated
rule based model.

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:33.190
So in that workflow call,
I'm binding together

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:35.530
my data processing
recipe, and I'm saying,

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:37.280
we're going to use
this with cubist.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:40.130
And then there's a simple fit
function we use after that.

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:41.830
And that does all
the preprocessing,

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:44.230
all the estimation that
happens during preprocessing

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:47.480
for this recipe hands
the data off to cubist.

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:50.290
It runs all its model and
it's all sitting in one object

00:17:50.290 --> 00:17:53.170
that you can save or
deploy, spoiler alert,

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:55.280
and do your things with.

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:58.390
And so that last blue block
is that one line for predict

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:00.170
that we'd like to be able to do.

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:02.460
And it does all the same
things for your new data.

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:04.210
So we want to try to
simplify this process

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:05.360
as much as possible.

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:07.480
I mean, that recipe is
not terribly simple,

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:09.810
but it's also a very like,

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:13.050
predictable,
tested, reliable way

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:15.210
to encapsulate
your preprocessing.

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:16.645
So when we think
about ergonomics,

00:18:16.645 --> 00:18:18.520
that's the kind of thing
we're talking about.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:21.330
We want to do potentially very
simple or potentially very

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:23.550
sophisticated things
with a really nice API

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:25.740
that we think you'll
enjoy working with.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.620
However, there may be parts
of the modeling development

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:31.260
process that maybe
aren't so ergonomic.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.690
Anything you would like
to mention about that, Julia?

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:36.540
Yeah, in my
experience and talking

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.580
with folks about
their modeling, one

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:42.690
of the parts of the whole
modeling analysis that

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:47.490
has been the least ergonomic
that has involved the most pain

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:51.570
is the process of getting
your model off your machine.

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:55.050
You know, you would
spend time training,

00:18:55.050 --> 00:19:00.120
tuning, developing a beautiful,
accurate, appropriate model.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:04.500
And then when it's time
to deploy that model,

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:06.990
to put that model
into production,

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:09.120
there has been a
real lack of tools

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:12.000
for people who are
excellent model

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:16.860
developers to be able to take
that model and get it off

00:19:16.860 --> 00:19:18.750
where it needs to go.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:21.970
Let's let's do another
poll here together.

00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:24.600
And let's take this time to
reflect a little bit on what

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.490
your experience has been like.

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:30.840
If you if you have deployed
a model to production,

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:34.830
think about what has
been what has gone well,

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:37.020
what has been what have
been the pain points,

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:39.720
whether that's the
code that you needed

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:43.920
to write that was awkward, or
the collaboration between you

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:48.070
and the software engineers or
IT folks in your organization.

00:19:48.070 --> 00:19:51.480
If you haven't deployed
a model to production,

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:53.140
reflect on why that is.

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:55.500
Is that because it really
isn't part of your job

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:57.730
like you don't need to
in your organization?

00:19:57.730 --> 00:20:01.650
Or is it because there
have been barriers?

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:04.260
There are - you -  maybe
you didn't know how

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:07.260
or maybe it was
difficult to collaborate

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:10.260
within your organization
to get your model where

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:11.730
it needs to go.

00:20:11.730 --> 00:20:14.800
Maybe some of you are sitting
here and you're like, what?

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:18.450
What does that even mean
for the word production?

00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:20.550
That's a great
question because there

00:20:20.550 --> 00:20:23.940
isn't some industry
standard definition

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:25.810
for what production means.

00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:30.510
I like to use the
definition or the idea

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:32.970
that putting a model
into production

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:36.090
is taking your trained
model, getting it

00:20:36.090 --> 00:20:39.030
out of the computational
environment where

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:41.790
you trained it and
putting it somewhere where

00:20:41.790 --> 00:20:46.180
it is useful to a
wider group of people.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:48.630
So by that definition,
taking a model

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:51.150
and putting it in a Shiny
app and letting people

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:53.310
interact to get
predictions, you know,

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:55.560
we can say that's production.

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:59.790
The industry standard these
days for model putting models

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:02.310
into production for
model deployment

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:08.190
is to create RESTful APIs so
that you can scale your API,

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:09.880
you can have it
as a microservice.

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:13.500
And we have this convenient way
to take a model that you have

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:16.230
trained and put it in a
computational environment

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:19.350
where it's accessible to
anyone in your organization

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:20.580
who needs it.

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:25.530
I've been working on a new
framework for model deployment

00:21:25.530 --> 00:21:29.010
and other ML ops
tasks called vetiver.

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:35.130
So if you are into perfumery
or fancy candles like I am,

00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:37.480
you may have seen this
word, this word vetiver.

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:42.850
So vetiver is a stabilizing
ingredient in perfume.

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:45.180
It takes, the more
volatile fragrances

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:47.080
and it stabilizes them.

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:51.600
So in this metaphor, your model
is this more volatile thing.

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:54.450
You have used
different hyperparameters,

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:57.360
you have used different
data, and vetiver

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:00.540
helps you stabilize it
so you can version it,

00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:03.330
deploy it and monitor it.

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:05.980
Let's walk through
that a little briefly.

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:09.300
So any modeling
process starts with you

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:12.460
collecting data, having
access to some kind of data.

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:17.500
The first thing you need to
do is to understand, clean,

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:20.880
you need to engage in the
process of exploratory data

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:21.910
analysis.

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:25.050
There are great tools
in Python and R

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:28.120
for you to approach those tasks.

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:31.860
Next, you want to
train your model,

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:34.720
you want to train,
tune, evaluate.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:37.680
And again, there are
amazing open source tools

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:40.620
like what we are talking
about to be able to approach

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.330
those kinds of tasks.

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:46.260
So far, at this point,
that becomes less true

00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:48.340
as we move around this cycle.

00:22:48.340 --> 00:22:50.820
Once your model is
trained, you need

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:56.370
to have a reliable scheme to
version that model so that you

00:22:56.370 --> 00:23:02.490
can know which version of my
model was used over which time

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:05.730
with each data, once
your model is versioned,

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:07.450
you need to deploy it.

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:09.670
And after it's
deployed, your job

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:10.700
is not done.

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:13.370
You need to also
monitor your model.

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:17.950
You need to measure
regularly the statistical,

00:23:17.950 --> 00:23:22.330
the statistical characteristics
of how your model is performing

00:23:22.330 --> 00:23:27.370
so that you can make
adjustments, decide to retrain,

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:28.910
head down that path.

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:34.570
So the tasks on the right
side of this diagram

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:38.330
are ones that there are
great open source tools for.

00:23:38.330 --> 00:23:40.630
The task on the
left side are ones

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:44.840
where that has not been true and
this is where vetiver sits - So,

00:23:44.840 --> 00:23:48.460
Vetiver is a framework
for versioning, deploying

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:50.860
and monitoring your model.

00:23:50.860 --> 00:23:54.310
Now you can probably
guess from this slide

00:23:54.310 --> 00:23:56.500
that vetiver is not
just for tidymodels,

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:58.810
it supports many
kinds of models in R,

00:23:58.810 --> 00:24:01.610
and Vetiver is not just for R.

00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:03.730
Vetiver was designed
from the beginning

00:24:03.730 --> 00:24:07.130
to have feature parity
between R and Python.

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:10.240
You can use the
tools that you want

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:16.070
to use to understand and clean
your data to train your model.

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:19.960
And then Vetiver can
support these ML ops tasks

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:22.210
for both Python and R.

00:24:22.210 --> 00:24:26.350
If you want to learn
more about what MLOps is

00:24:26.350 --> 00:24:30.040
and how Vetiver works with
a specifically with a Python

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:33.700
flavor, I invite you to come
to the talk of my coworker

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:37.630
Isabel Zimmerman, which is in
the next session about MLOps

00:24:37.630 --> 00:24:38.680
and Vetiver.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:40.450
While you're here
though, I'll just

00:24:40.450 --> 00:24:42.620
show you what this looks
like in R a little bit.

00:24:42.620 --> 00:24:47.110
So let's say I, I trained that
model that Max talked about

00:24:47.110 --> 00:24:49.040
and now it's time to deploy it.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:54.560
The first step is to create
a deployable model object.

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:57.340
This collects all
the information

00:24:57.340 --> 00:25:00.790
that we need to be able to
move this model from where

00:25:00.790 --> 00:25:04.600
I trained it over to a new
computational environment

00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:06.380
where I can make predictions.

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:11.260
This information includes
the types and number

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:14.540
and names of the predictors,
the original predictors.

00:25:14.540 --> 00:25:18.460
This includes also the
packages and package versions,

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:20.860
the specific software
that is needed

00:25:20.860 --> 00:25:23.800
to be installed over
in that other place

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:25.760
to be able to make predictions.

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:28.480
Once we have that
deployable model object,

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:34.370
the process of spinning up
an API is quite ergonomic.

00:25:34.370 --> 00:25:35.660
This was our goal here.

00:25:35.660 --> 00:25:38.590
So on the R side, we
use the Plumber package

00:25:38.590 --> 00:25:42.460
and once you have a
Plumber router initialized,

00:25:42.460 --> 00:25:45.070
it is literally just
one line of code

00:25:45.070 --> 00:25:48.700
to spin up your model
aware API that's

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:53.500
ready to make, to serve
predictions at an endpoint.

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:55.580
So this is what we
mean by ergonomic.

00:25:55.580 --> 00:25:57.670
We want to let
you be expressive,

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:01.340
to let you be fluent, to
let you get your job done

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:02.420
in this way.

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:04.450
Now, let's move on
to our second point,

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:09.220
which is how tidymodels
makes you more effective.

00:26:09.220 --> 00:26:11.800
Max, what when we think about
this like being effective,

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:14.740
what are some of the things
that come to mind for you?

00:26:14.740 --> 00:26:18.100
So, you know, back when I used
to do modeling for a living,

00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:20.500
I feel like I was most
effective when somebody

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:22.000
brought some data to me.

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:24.010
Like to know of like a new project

00:26:24.010 --> 00:26:27.100
and I was able to give them
a predictive model back that

00:26:27.100 --> 00:26:29.530
really like met or exceeded
what they were interested in

00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:31.835
or what they needed for
the problem and not take,

00:26:31.835 --> 00:26:33.710
let's say, like months
to be able to do that.

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:36.580
So for me, effective is
being able to produce

00:26:36.580 --> 00:26:40.480
a performant model for
whatever the context is

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:42.520
or whatever the requirements
are for the project

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:44.177
and do that pretty efficiently.

00:26:44.177 --> 00:26:45.760
So, you know, what
we'll talk about in

00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:48.820
a minute are some
sort of advanced tools

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:50.870
that we can give
you to help do that.

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:54.340
Some are about performance,
some are about efficiency.

00:26:54.340 --> 00:26:56.407
A big part of building
a machine learning model

00:26:56.407 --> 00:26:58.240
is model tuning, which
we mentioned earlier,

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:00.520
where you have these
hyperparameters that you can't

00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:02.353
directly estimate from
the data and you need

00:27:02.353 --> 00:27:04.270
to find a way to tune them.

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:05.860
So another poll here.

00:27:05.860 --> 00:27:07.810
Have you ever tuned a model?

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:10.940
So, for example, if you
have a neural network.

00:27:10.940 --> 00:27:12.940
And you want to figure
out how many hidden units

00:27:12.940 --> 00:27:15.010
or hidden layers
you should have,

00:27:15.010 --> 00:27:18.940
you need to do some sort of
methodology to figure out what

00:27:18.940 --> 00:27:22.400
a good, what good values are
for those tuning parameters.

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:24.580
So have you never done that?

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:28.750
Yes and I don't want to
ever do that again situation.

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:31.390
Maybe you did it in a worked
well or you do it all the time

00:27:31.390 --> 00:27:33.970
and it was easy, or you're
a pro, so we definitely

00:27:33.970 --> 00:27:35.270
want to get feedback from you.

00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:37.270
We don't get the feedback
from the Slido polls

00:27:37.270 --> 00:27:38.060
as we do them.

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:39.700
But honestly, for
this in the next one,

00:27:39.700 --> 00:27:42.010
I'm kind of dying to see
what your responses are.

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:45.400
So go ahead and let us know
what you think about that.

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:47.650
So just as an example
of some techniques

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:49.588
to talk about
effectiveness, you know,

00:27:49.588 --> 00:27:51.880
there's a lot of things that
aren't like bleeding edge,

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:53.710
but they're relatively
cutting edge

00:27:53.710 --> 00:27:56.280
and might be coming from
different parts of literature.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:58.090
Some things we
include in tiny models

00:27:58.090 --> 00:28:00.000
are from the deep
learning literature.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:01.180
There's actually some
interesting things

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:02.800
we're doing from the
computational chemistry

00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:04.960
literature that we're using
for other types of data

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:06.410
or any type of data.

00:28:06.410 --> 00:28:09.490
Some examples of that would
be feature embedding methods,

00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:12.040
and these are just really
fancy machine learning methods

00:28:12.040 --> 00:28:13.700
for dimensionality reduction.

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:16.698
So UMAP and isoMap, like
multidimensional scaling

00:28:16.698 --> 00:28:18.490
are pretty powerful and
interesting methods,

00:28:18.490 --> 00:28:20.698
and we'd like you to be able
to use them for any data

00:28:20.698 --> 00:28:22.580
and use them with any model.

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:26.110
So we want to enable you
to use those pretty easily.

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:28.750
The effect encoding is
the type of methodology

00:28:28.750 --> 00:28:31.810
might use, like with the artist
field and the Spotify data

00:28:31.810 --> 00:28:34.240
where you have a large
number of values,

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:36.220
you need to find an
efficient and practical way

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:37.462
of doing that.

00:28:37.462 --> 00:28:39.670
You know, that's not a
terribly sophisticated method,

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:41.230
but if you were
doing that before,

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:43.870
you would have had to write
a bunch of other stuff

00:28:43.870 --> 00:28:47.650
in your script to do that
before and after modeling.

00:28:47.650 --> 00:28:50.920
And we'll talk about model
tuning just a little bit more.

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:54.820
Bayesian optimization is a tool
that's pretty, pretty common

00:28:54.820 --> 00:28:56.650
in the deep learning
or neural network side,

00:28:56.650 --> 00:28:58.345
but you can use
that for anything.

00:28:58.345 --> 00:29:00.220
There are a few packages
in order to do that,

00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:02.532
but they're not really
well integrated overall

00:29:02.532 --> 00:29:04.990
with all the types of models.
We provide you tuning methods

00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:08.020
for Bayesian optimization you
could use for your boosted tree

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:10.960
or for support vector machine
or whatever you're going to do.

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:12.760
And then racing is
an example we'll look

00:29:12.760 --> 00:29:14.240
at in a little bit more detail.

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:17.060
It's a way to do really,
really efficient grid search.

00:29:17.060 --> 00:29:19.788
So in grid search you might
come up with for your tuning

00:29:19.788 --> 00:29:21.580
parameters, you might
come up with a number

00:29:21.580 --> 00:29:24.370
of different candidate
like values of their tuning

00:29:24.370 --> 00:29:25.043
parameters.

00:29:25.043 --> 00:29:26.710
So you might say,
let's try seven hidden

00:29:26.710 --> 00:29:28.570
units and 12 hidden
units and two

00:29:28.570 --> 00:29:30.860
and so on, but you
pre-define them.

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:33.040
And the problem with
grid search sometimes

00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:35.470
is you don't know if some
of those choices you made

00:29:35.470 --> 00:29:37.990
about the candidate parameters
are any good until you're

00:29:37.990 --> 00:29:39.650
done with all the computations.

00:29:39.650 --> 00:29:43.630
And so what racing does is it's
a dynamic way of doing that,

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:45.400
is as you start to
do the model tuning,

00:29:45.400 --> 00:29:46.900
it's starting to
look at the results

00:29:46.900 --> 00:29:49.780
as they happen and look at some
tuning parameter combinations

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:52.310
and say, oh, those are
never going to be the best.

00:29:52.310 --> 00:29:54.160
So it does some
statistics to like estimate

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:57.130
the probability that something
will be the winner in this race

00:29:57.130 --> 00:29:59.890
and it lets you do
things very efficiently.

00:29:59.890 --> 00:30:02.230
So in this little
animation we have here,

00:30:02.230 --> 00:30:05.530
the y-axis is 50 different
model configurations

00:30:05.530 --> 00:30:08.050
for a machine learning
model, and the x-axis

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:09.740
is a measurement of performance.

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:12.430
And so the blue dot, if you
can see it there, near the top,

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:15.670
is the current winner
during each re resample

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:18.410
that we're doing
for our grid search.

00:30:18.410 --> 00:30:21.880
And as you can see, a lot
of these candidate values

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:24.435
got grayed out and were
eliminated on the next step.

00:30:24.435 --> 00:30:25.810
So every time it
does a resample,

00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:28.270
it's doing some analysis
to figure out what's good

00:30:28.270 --> 00:30:30.980
and what's bad and it
gets rid of the bad stuff.

00:30:30.980 --> 00:30:34.510
And so really quickly after
like eight or 10 resamples,

00:30:34.510 --> 00:30:37.610
you're down to just a
handful of models out of 50.

00:30:37.610 --> 00:30:40.152
And in this particular
case, of all the models

00:30:40.152 --> 00:30:41.860
you could have fit,
you would be actually

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:43.772
only fitting about 7% of them.

00:30:43.772 --> 00:30:45.730
And if you're working
with parallel processing,

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:48.040
that's like almost like
a three-fold increase

00:30:48.040 --> 00:30:48.623
in efficiency.

00:30:48.623 --> 00:30:50.623
So it took you a third
of the time it would have

00:30:50.623 --> 00:30:52.225
done with regular grid search.

00:30:52.225 --> 00:30:54.100
And if you weren't using
parallel processing,

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:57.070
it's a nine times as fast
as what you could have

00:30:57.070 --> 00:30:58.563
done with regular grid search.

00:30:58.563 --> 00:30:59.980
So these are
examples of the types

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:01.688
of things that we want
to implement, like

00:31:01.688 --> 00:31:03.940
we're giving you APIs for
them that are pretty simple,

00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:06.370
let you control a lot of
things, but will make it easier

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:10.030
for you to go further and
faster with your modeling.

00:31:10.030 --> 00:31:13.660
This racing example
is such a great one

00:31:13.660 --> 00:31:18.370
because it really highlights
how tidymodels is extensible.

00:31:18.370 --> 00:31:24.490
The package that provides that
infrastructure is not a core

00:31:24.490 --> 00:31:25.820
tidymodels package,

00:31:25.820 --> 00:31:28.460
but an extension package.

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:31.750
So the metaphor that we can
think about here together

00:31:31.750 --> 00:31:33.260
is one of LEGOs.

00:31:33.260 --> 00:31:37.210
So if you have one
beautiful LEGO block here,

00:31:37.210 --> 00:31:38.540
we can admire it.

00:31:38.540 --> 00:31:40.910
Look, look how
well it's designed.

00:31:40.910 --> 00:31:43.300
But the reason why LEGOs
are fun to play with

00:31:43.300 --> 00:31:44.890
is that you can
put them together

00:31:44.890 --> 00:31:48.160
and you can put them together
in the way that meets your needs

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:52.360
or fulfills your vision, or is
what it is that you want to -

00:31:52.360 --> 00:31:54.320
what is what you want to build.

00:31:54.320 --> 00:31:57.080
And that is how tidymodels works.

00:31:57.080 --> 00:31:58.660
We showed a, you
know, a handful

00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:02.020
of hex stickers up before that
were some core tidymodels

00:32:02.020 --> 00:32:04.500
packages, but -

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:06.390
and said, you know,
this is tidymodels,

00:32:06.390 --> 00:32:09.900
but if we ask the question,
what else is tidymodels?

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:12.660
There's another whole
group of packages that

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:15.870
are for our more specialized tasks.

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:18.180
I'm just going to run
through the ones on the top

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:20.160
there briefly.

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:24.480
Text recipes is a feature
engineering extension package

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:28.130
for text preprocessing
text feature engineering.

00:32:28.130 --> 00:32:30.250
This is one of my favorite
things to talk about.

00:32:30.250 --> 00:32:33.390
And tidymodels
gives you great tools

00:32:33.390 --> 00:32:36.750
to make you effective
in building text models.

00:32:36.750 --> 00:32:39.060
The next one there is
the censored package,

00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:41.950
which is for survival analysis.

00:32:41.950 --> 00:32:44.790
Our coworker Hannah is
going to be giving a talk

00:32:44.790 --> 00:32:46.150
on that in the next session,

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:49.380
if you particularly work
with survival analysis.

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:51.510
The next one is
interesting, stacks,

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:56.910
because it builds on top of
the whole tidymodels framework

00:32:56.910 --> 00:33:01.350
to actually to take multiple
different kinds of models

00:33:01.350 --> 00:33:04.470
and stack them together
or ensemble them

00:33:04.470 --> 00:33:09.480
so that you can squeeze
that last bit of performance

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:11.800
out of your data that you have

00:33:11.800 --> 00:33:13.740
if that's something
that is important

00:33:13.740 --> 00:33:16.010
in your particular use case.

00:33:16.010 --> 00:33:18.460
The last one up
there is modeltime.

00:33:18.460 --> 00:33:21.120
And I want to highlight
this because this one is

00:33:21.120 --> 00:33:23.850
a package that's not
built by us who work

00:33:23.850 --> 00:33:25.270
on the tidymodels team.

00:33:25.270 --> 00:33:27.940
It's actually built by a
member of our community.

00:33:27.940 --> 00:33:31.890
So this is built by Matt Dancho,
who is here somewhere today

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:35.310
and it highlights how
tidymodels is extensible,

00:33:35.310 --> 00:33:39.420
not just by us but also by you.

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:41.820
This is true whether you're
interested in building

00:33:41.820 --> 00:33:45.690
some open source software
for a new kind of model

00:33:45.690 --> 00:33:47.410
or a new kind of resampling.

00:33:47.410 --> 00:33:51.240
It's also true, just within
your own organization,

00:33:51.240 --> 00:33:54.870
you can build, for
example, a custom metric

00:33:54.870 --> 00:33:58.290
based on your
company's KPIs and use

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:03.120
that to optimize the models
that you are training.

00:34:03.120 --> 00:34:07.470
Now, when we start talking
about all these packages,

00:34:07.470 --> 00:34:09.780
one of the things that we
start to hear from people

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:12.570
is a bit of discomfort
with the fact

00:34:12.570 --> 00:34:14.500
that there are so many packages.

00:34:14.500 --> 00:34:16.870
How do I know which
one does what?

00:34:16.870 --> 00:34:18.190
How do I find them?

00:34:18.190 --> 00:34:20.400
And we want to acknowledge
that, that yeah, there,

00:34:20.400 --> 00:34:24.580
there is this little bit
of a learning process.

00:34:24.580 --> 00:34:28.560
And it's important to have
tools in your, in your tool belt

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:31.409
to be able to identify
what functions

00:34:31.409 --> 00:34:33.150
come from what packages.

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:37.050
However, we really want to
emphasize that this modularity

00:34:37.050 --> 00:34:39.460
makes all of our lives better.

00:34:39.460 --> 00:34:42.540
It makes our lives better as
maintainers of the packages,

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:46.260
and it makes your lives better
as users of the package.

00:34:46.260 --> 00:34:49.739
Smaller, more
modular packages can

00:34:49.739 --> 00:34:52.000
be released more frequently.

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:55.380
With smaller changes, we
can more quickly fix bugs,

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:57.990
so it makes our lives
quite a bit better.

00:34:57.990 --> 00:35:00.270
It makes your lives
better directly,

00:35:00.270 --> 00:35:02.760
not only because we're doing
a better job maintaining,

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:04.300
but actually directly.

00:35:04.300 --> 00:35:07.170
And this is, I would
say, most highlighted

00:35:07.170 --> 00:35:10.140
when we are talking
about model deployment.

00:35:10.140 --> 00:35:12.510
Let's say we train the
model using that racing

00:35:12.510 --> 00:35:15.070
method that Max shared with us.

00:35:15.070 --> 00:35:19.110
We don't need any of that
racing infrastructure when

00:35:19.110 --> 00:35:20.770
we go to deploy our model.

00:35:20.770 --> 00:35:24.310
In fact, we don't need the
tuning infrastructure at all.

00:35:24.310 --> 00:35:27.570
We need a subset of
the modeling software

00:35:27.570 --> 00:35:29.970
that is required to
make a prediction.

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:33.330
Our packages being
modular, allows

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:37.350
you to make smaller
docker images,

00:35:37.350 --> 00:35:41.100
have far faster
installation times,

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:46.710
being able to have more
scalable models in production.

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:49.230
So, so far we've
talked about how we

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:52.780
can have a more ergonomic
modeling process.

00:35:52.780 --> 00:35:55.140
We have talked about
how we can make

00:35:55.140 --> 00:35:57.940
you more effective in the
models that you build.

00:35:57.940 --> 00:36:01.410
And last, we want to
talk about being safe,

00:36:01.410 --> 00:36:04.030
practicing safe
machine learning.

00:36:04.030 --> 00:36:08.010
So, Max, how does tidymodels
keep us safe?

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:10.380
So the whole idea of
safety in modeling, you

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:14.430
might be like, well, what do you
mean by safety or being safe?

00:36:14.430 --> 00:36:17.490
It turns out with like complex
machine learning models

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.050
and especially complex
data, it's possible for you

00:36:20.050 --> 00:36:22.620
to do something
horribly wrong and not

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:25.930
really know it until a
really inopportune time.

00:36:25.930 --> 00:36:27.763
And I've made this
confession several times

00:36:27.763 --> 00:36:29.680
at different conferences
and things like that.

00:36:29.680 --> 00:36:31.320
But, you know, in
my first job, I

00:36:31.320 --> 00:36:35.017
had a project that was like
3/4 of the total R&amp;D budget.

00:36:35.017 --> 00:36:37.350
You know, we were doing a
bunch of sophisticated machine

00:36:37.350 --> 00:36:39.660
learning with like a large
number of predictors,

00:36:39.660 --> 00:36:41.972
and we thought we'd
gotten it to a good point.

00:36:41.972 --> 00:36:43.930
And my boss came by and
he said, how's it look?

00:36:43.930 --> 00:36:46.900
And I'm like, accuracy is like,
like 90%. It's like, great.

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:49.260
And, and then we
got more samples in

00:36:49.260 --> 00:36:50.740
and we missed them all.

00:36:50.740 --> 00:36:53.130
And we did a lot of soul
searching in the days

00:36:53.130 --> 00:36:55.320
after that and figured out
some methodology mistakes

00:36:55.320 --> 00:36:58.170
that we made that others make,
just to be honest with you

00:36:58.170 --> 00:36:59.490
quite often.

00:36:59.490 --> 00:37:03.770
And once we figure that out
and fixed it, we, we had more

00:37:03.770 --> 00:37:05.610
genuine estimates performance.

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:08.930
So there are times in
modeling where, especially

00:37:08.930 --> 00:37:11.090
if you're doing
something complex,

00:37:11.090 --> 00:37:13.610
that you might
accidentally fool yourself

00:37:13.610 --> 00:37:15.955
into believing you have
something that's really good,

00:37:15.955 --> 00:37:18.080
but you have something
that's actually not as good,

00:37:18.080 --> 00:37:20.450
and you won't figure that
out until we get new data.

00:37:20.450 --> 00:37:23.030
And here's one of the
reasons is going back

00:37:23.030 --> 00:37:25.310
to this part of
our previous slides

00:37:25.310 --> 00:37:27.260
is we have all
the modeling parts

00:37:27.260 --> 00:37:30.110
and those are pretty well
understood, how to validate them

00:37:30.110 --> 00:37:31.620
and how to assess them.

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:34.040
But very often before
we get to the model,

00:37:34.040 --> 00:37:36.500
we might do something
either simple or something

00:37:36.500 --> 00:37:39.290
really complex to the data
that involves something

00:37:39.290 --> 00:37:42.450
that's not like a deterministic,
like data cleaning operation.

00:37:42.450 --> 00:37:45.010
We might be doing some
statistical estimation, right?

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:46.802
So remember you're
talking about the effect

00:37:46.802 --> 00:37:48.620
encoding or measuring
the redundancies

00:37:48.620 --> 00:37:49.800
between predictors.

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:53.510
And so it's really, really
important in some cases

00:37:53.510 --> 00:37:55.460
that we handle that
in the right way

00:37:55.460 --> 00:37:57.980
to make sure that our
statistics that tell us

00:37:57.980 --> 00:37:59.552
how well our model
is doing, take

00:37:59.552 --> 00:38:01.760
into account the good and
bad parts that might happen

00:38:01.760 --> 00:38:03.440
in that part of the system.

00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:06.690
So let's think about
our Spotify script.

00:38:06.690 --> 00:38:09.440
We have all these genres,
maybe we got rid of redundancy,

00:38:09.440 --> 00:38:12.740
but maybe we want to refine that
feature set a little bit more

00:38:12.740 --> 00:38:14.820
before we give it to our model.

00:38:14.820 --> 00:38:17.690
And let's say we want to do
some fancy supervised feature

00:38:17.690 --> 00:38:18.840
selection routine.

00:38:18.840 --> 00:38:21.410
So we might want to pick
the top 10 variables

00:38:21.410 --> 00:38:23.720
to give to the model and
pick those 10 because they're

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:25.880
the most influential
for the outcome,

00:38:25.880 --> 00:38:27.720
like for popularity
in this case.

00:38:27.720 --> 00:38:30.290
So that's like a feature, a
supervised feature selection

00:38:30.290 --> 00:38:31.050
technique.

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:32.750
And so let's say in
our Spotify data,

00:38:32.750 --> 00:38:34.790
we're going to do
10-fold cross validation

00:38:34.790 --> 00:38:38.460
to validate our model and we're
going to use feature selection.

00:38:38.460 --> 00:38:40.400
So actually, how
many times do you

00:38:40.400 --> 00:38:43.010
think we're going to run
that feature selection or are

00:38:43.010 --> 00:38:44.930
we going to run it once?

00:38:44.930 --> 00:38:47.420
Are we going to run it 10 times?

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:50.018
We could run it 11
times or we could

00:38:50.018 --> 00:38:51.560
run it some fractional
amount of time

00:38:51.560 --> 00:38:53.910
because it was taking so
long that you just like hash.

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:55.285
You know, you slam
the Escape key

00:38:55.285 --> 00:38:58.550
until it stopped after
three iterations.

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:01.652
I want to see the results
of these later on today.

00:39:01.652 --> 00:39:03.110
And it's not like
a trick question,

00:39:03.110 --> 00:39:05.720
but there's actually
more than one of these

00:39:05.720 --> 00:39:06.600
could be true.

00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:08.142
And I'll talk about
that in a second.

00:39:11.580 --> 00:39:14.400
So here's the situation
we're in is we have some data,

00:39:14.400 --> 00:39:16.770
we have a list of predictors.

00:39:16.770 --> 00:39:18.900
We want to enact some
sort of feature selection

00:39:18.900 --> 00:39:21.120
routine to filter out
some of those predictors

00:39:21.120 --> 00:39:22.510
before we give it to our model.

00:39:22.510 --> 00:39:25.740
Let's just say for kicks we're
doing ordinary least squares

00:39:25.740 --> 00:39:26.670
with lm.

00:39:26.670 --> 00:39:29.020
And so that produces some
sort of fitted model.

00:39:29.020 --> 00:39:31.197
And that red box
there is basically

00:39:31.197 --> 00:39:33.030
the box that says like,
what am I estimating

00:39:33.030 --> 00:39:36.570
and what should I validate here
in the way we have the diagram

00:39:36.570 --> 00:39:39.180
here treats the
feature selection bit

00:39:39.180 --> 00:39:41.870
as if it were sort
of outside the model.

00:39:41.870 --> 00:39:43.620
So if I were to ask
most people like where

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:45.787
are we modeling in this
diagram, they would probably

00:39:45.787 --> 00:39:49.860
do what we show and just circle
the actual technical model

00:39:49.860 --> 00:39:51.310
part of the process.

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:54.100
But in actuality, what you have
to do is you have to do this.

00:39:54.100 --> 00:39:56.700
And in this previous
slide, this is

00:39:56.700 --> 00:39:59.490
what I did when I made that
horrible mistake in my job

00:39:59.490 --> 00:40:02.670
is I just selected the features
once, put them into the model,

00:40:02.670 --> 00:40:05.310
and there's a big sort
of circular argument

00:40:05.310 --> 00:40:07.230
methodology wise in
doing that and it gave me

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:08.470
really bad results.

00:40:08.470 --> 00:40:10.090
What we want to do is this.

00:40:10.090 --> 00:40:12.330
So the answer to
this is probably

00:40:12.330 --> 00:40:15.810
C where for every one of
those 10-fold cross validations,

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:18.370
you do the feature selection
over and over again.

00:40:18.370 --> 00:40:21.225
And that may seem like excessive
or it may take a long time,

00:40:21.225 --> 00:40:23.850
but at the same time, you can't
measure the effect of something

00:40:23.850 --> 00:40:26.170
that's not within your
sort of validation system.

00:40:26.170 --> 00:40:30.180
If it's outside of it, it
might give you bad answers.

00:40:30.180 --> 00:40:33.190
So the answer is
probably B, which is 10.

00:40:33.190 --> 00:40:36.090
But let's say this model
was really, really good

00:40:36.090 --> 00:40:36.887
and you liked it.

00:40:36.887 --> 00:40:38.970
And compared to all the
other things that you did,

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:40.720
this is the one you want
to take to production.

00:40:40.720 --> 00:40:42.400
You want to put it in
Vetiver and deploy it.

00:40:42.400 --> 00:40:43.942
Well, actually, what
you would end up

00:40:43.942 --> 00:40:45.900
doing is doing it in 11
time, because to build

00:40:45.900 --> 00:40:48.180
the final model, you have
to do the feature section

00:40:48.180 --> 00:40:52.230
on the entire data set
hand that data over to lm

00:40:52.230 --> 00:40:54.400
and run lm on the
entire data set.

00:40:54.400 --> 00:40:57.510
So really the answer
there is either B or C,

00:40:57.510 --> 00:41:00.163
but it's highlighting that there
are some pitfalls and gotchas.

00:41:00.163 --> 00:41:01.830
That is really easy
for somebody like me

00:41:01.830 --> 00:41:03.360
who studied this
in graduate school

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:04.740
and did it for a
living for a while,

00:41:04.740 --> 00:41:06.573
that you could actually
make these mistakes.

00:41:06.573 --> 00:41:09.728
Unfortunately, it
happens quite a bit.

00:41:09.728 --> 00:41:12.270
So, you know, there's quite a
few papers that talk about this

00:41:12.270 --> 00:41:15.000
and measure this, but there's
one that just came out,

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:17.417
you know, we're bringing it
up because their findings were

00:41:17.417 --> 00:41:18.690
maybe like not great.

00:41:18.690 --> 00:41:21.310
So leakage in this
context is data leakage.

00:41:21.310 --> 00:41:23.860
And it's really where
you're using the wrong data.

00:41:23.860 --> 00:41:27.010
It may be the wrong time to
do some sort of calculation.

00:41:27.010 --> 00:41:29.820
Right so in my case, I was
doing feature selection

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:32.580
at the wrong time
with data that maybe I

00:41:32.580 --> 00:41:33.820
shouldn't have been using.

00:41:33.820 --> 00:41:35.520
And so in this paper,
what they do is

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:38.287
they look at a lot of
different publications.

00:41:38.287 --> 00:41:39.870
And you can tell
based on their method

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:41.120
sections what they were doing.

00:41:41.120 --> 00:41:43.170
And there was a lot of
situations where they're

00:41:43.170 --> 00:41:45.637
doing like the bad methodology.

00:41:45.637 --> 00:41:47.220
And in tidymodels,
what we want to do

00:41:47.220 --> 00:41:48.810
is whether people
know it or not is

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:52.170
we sort of want to silently
sort of give you guardrails.

00:41:52.170 --> 00:41:54.060
So if you follow the
process of tidy models

00:41:54.060 --> 00:41:56.970
and use a tiny model syntax,
it's very, very, very difficult

00:41:56.970 --> 00:41:58.390
to do the wrong thing.

00:41:58.390 --> 00:42:01.660
So just to give you an example
of that, here's another recipe.

00:42:01.660 --> 00:42:04.290
We're going to use a recipe
extension package written

00:42:04.290 --> 00:42:06.540
by somebody in the community
called recipe selectors,

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:09.210
and that has a recipe
in it that will do like,

00:42:09.210 --> 00:42:11.700
let's say like a random forest
variable importance score

00:42:11.700 --> 00:42:13.120
across your predictors.

00:42:13.120 --> 00:42:16.650
And then you can select the
top 10 or 15 or 30, whatever

00:42:16.650 --> 00:42:19.435
you want you to think you
should do of the most predictors

00:42:19.435 --> 00:42:21.810
and give that to, let's say,
your, your linear regression

00:42:21.810 --> 00:42:24.370
model, but we don't really
know how many we should pick.

00:42:24.370 --> 00:42:27.150
So if you look at
that line there, it

00:42:27.150 --> 00:42:30.370
has an argument called
tune or I'm sorry, top_p

00:42:30.370 --> 00:42:32.710
So it's like how many predictors
should I retain?

00:42:32.710 --> 00:42:35.210
We give that a value of
tune and that value of tune

00:42:35.210 --> 00:42:36.960
marks it in tidy models
as being something

00:42:36.960 --> 00:42:38.050
that we want to optimize.

00:42:38.050 --> 00:42:39.690
We want to tune that parameter.

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:41.580
And so then we can take
our new recipe that

00:42:41.580 --> 00:42:44.820
built on our previous Spotify
recipe, put that in a workflow

00:42:44.820 --> 00:42:46.403
with a modeling
function that says

00:42:46.403 --> 00:42:48.570
we're going to use just
plain old linear regression,

00:42:48.570 --> 00:42:51.420
and we could use the
tune grid function

00:42:51.420 --> 00:42:54.540
to do a grid search to find
a good value for how many

00:42:54.540 --> 00:42:56.230
of those predictors
that we should use.

00:42:56.230 --> 00:42:57.540
And so this is how you
would do the feature

00:42:57.540 --> 00:42:58.750
selection in tidymodels.

00:42:58.750 --> 00:43:02.010
Now the thing is, the way
we've set up sampling and data

00:43:02.010 --> 00:43:04.290
splitting, the way
we're combining our pre

00:43:04.290 --> 00:43:07.320
processor with our model,
it's nearly impossible

00:43:07.320 --> 00:43:10.320
if you accidentally do the wrong
thing here and inappropriately

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:13.920
validate or estimate
things from your data.

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:16.110
So we're really,
without really saying

00:43:16.110 --> 00:43:21.780
that, we're sort of forcing
that methodology for users.

00:43:21.780 --> 00:43:26.010
That idea of making it
hard to do the wrong thing

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:29.910
leads us to wanting to talk
about one more metaphor here.

00:43:29.910 --> 00:43:33.750
When we're here talking
about being on the road,

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:35.820
being on this journey
as we drive down,

00:43:35.820 --> 00:43:39.270
this may not be 100%
resonant to those of you

00:43:39.270 --> 00:43:42.180
who are visiting here from
outside the United States,

00:43:42.180 --> 00:43:43.830
maybe where you
live, somewhere where

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:46.180
you have excellent
public transportation.

00:43:46.180 --> 00:43:47.790
But I have always
lived somewhere

00:43:47.790 --> 00:43:52.060
where I rely on a car, like I
rely on a car to get around.

00:43:52.060 --> 00:43:56.910
And the idea of locking
my keys in my car

00:43:56.910 --> 00:44:00.270
is something that is kind of
terrifying because it's like,

00:44:00.270 --> 00:44:01.780
oh, no, what do I do now?

00:44:01.780 --> 00:44:03.040
I'm stuck somewhere.

00:44:03.040 --> 00:44:06.160
So here together, let's,
let's answer another poll.

00:44:06.160 --> 00:44:09.420
So when was the
last time that you

00:44:09.420 --> 00:44:11.330
locked your keys in your car?

00:44:11.330 --> 00:44:12.970
Was it pretty recently?

00:44:12.970 --> 00:44:14.540
Was it a long time ago.

00:44:14.540 --> 00:44:16.610
Have you have you
never done this?

00:44:16.610 --> 00:44:19.400
Max, when was the last time you
locked your keys in your car?

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:21.560
I would pick D
about 10 years ago.

00:44:21.560 --> 00:44:23.160
Yeah, I would pick D too.

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:25.490
It was like 10 or 15 years
ago was the last time

00:44:25.490 --> 00:44:28.940
that I locked my keys in my car
and I wouldn't be surprised.

00:44:28.940 --> 00:44:31.968
I have a bunch of people here
choose that they have never

00:44:31.968 --> 00:44:34.010
locked their keys in their
car, and I don't think

00:44:34.010 --> 00:44:37.550
it's because that much more
responsible or good at handling

00:44:37.550 --> 00:44:39.510
keys than we are,
but rather that

00:44:39.510 --> 00:44:43.370
you're quite a bit younger
than Max and I are.

00:44:43.370 --> 00:44:46.490
Because the thing is,
the car that I have now,

00:44:46.490 --> 00:44:49.820
it's really hard to
lock my keys in my car.

00:44:49.820 --> 00:44:53.750
Now, the car itself
is built in such a way

00:44:53.750 --> 00:44:57.450
that it protects against
common failure modes.

00:44:57.450 --> 00:45:00.510
So this I - this is how
tidymodels is built.

00:45:00.510 --> 00:45:05.060
It is built to protect you
against these common pitfalls,

00:45:05.060 --> 00:45:09.860
potholes that are on your road
to developing and deploying

00:45:09.860 --> 00:45:11.000
your model.

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:16.220
So the idea of keys is like
an immediately painful problem

00:45:16.220 --> 00:45:20.210
and that some problems that you
run into in machine learning

00:45:20.210 --> 00:45:24.080
are like that. One I
might suggest here

00:45:24.080 --> 00:45:26.790
would be the model deployment.

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:28.860
You know, you're like,
oh, it's, I can't get it.

00:45:28.860 --> 00:45:32.240
It fails every time I try to
push it to wherever I'm going.

00:45:32.240 --> 00:45:34.700
There are other kinds of
problems, though, that

00:45:34.700 --> 00:45:36.930
are not obvious until later.

00:45:36.930 --> 00:45:38.960
So the metaphor
we might use here

00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:41.630
would be not filling
your car up with gas

00:45:41.630 --> 00:45:42.960
and just driving along.

00:45:42.960 --> 00:45:45.080
And because you didn't
make that choice

00:45:45.080 --> 00:45:47.360
at the last exit
that had gas, you

00:45:47.360 --> 00:45:49.490
end up running out of gas later.

00:45:49.490 --> 00:45:52.700
tidymodels also protects
you against problems

00:45:52.700 --> 00:45:56.850
like that.  That you make some decision
during your modeling process.

00:45:56.850 --> 00:45:59.570
And it may not come to
bite you until you're

00:45:59.570 --> 00:46:01.730
predicting on new data.

00:46:01.730 --> 00:46:04.340
So as we come up and
start to wrap up here,

00:46:04.340 --> 00:46:08.660
we want to share a thought
from my friend and co-author

00:46:08.660 --> 00:46:12.860
Dave Robinson, who started
using tidymodels about a year ago

00:46:12.860 --> 00:46:17.090
and wrote a blog post
reflecting on his experience.

00:46:17.090 --> 00:46:21.500
So he said that, you know,
he, like us, sometimes

00:46:21.500 --> 00:46:24.530
hears some resistance
to the idea of

00:46:24.530 --> 00:46:28.290
or how tidy models works
that it makes it too easy.

00:46:28.290 --> 00:46:30.110
You know, people
you're not really

00:46:30.110 --> 00:46:32.430
thinking about what it
is that you're doing.

00:46:32.430 --> 00:46:37.470
But like, Dave, we think
this is entirely backwards.

00:46:37.470 --> 00:46:39.060
This is entirely backwards.

00:46:39.060 --> 00:46:43.370
We want to protect you as
a modeling practitioner

00:46:43.370 --> 00:46:48.650
from making silent
but bad choices so

00:46:48.650 --> 00:46:51.720
that you don't have to worry
and stress about those things.

00:46:51.720 --> 00:46:55.550
And you can focus on the
scientific and statistical

00:46:55.550 --> 00:46:57.800
questions that are the
reason why you're training

00:46:57.800 --> 00:46:59.850
a model in the first place.

00:46:59.850 --> 00:47:01.820
So, Max, here we are.

00:47:01.820 --> 00:47:03.620
What does this mean?

00:47:03.620 --> 00:47:06.920
Well, I think it means that
we're at the point with tidymodels where

00:47:06.920 --> 00:47:08.570
it's mature enough.

00:47:08.570 --> 00:47:11.120
The syntax is pretty much solid.

00:47:11.120 --> 00:47:12.705
No super major
changes we're going

00:47:12.705 --> 00:47:13.830
to be doing any minute now

00:47:13.830 --> 00:47:15.380
that would affect users.

00:47:15.380 --> 00:47:17.810
You may have noticed the
flurry of CRAN submissions

00:47:17.810 --> 00:47:19.975
where we've revved
everything to 1.0.

00:47:19.975 --> 00:47:22.100
So I think the message we
want to get across to you

00:47:22.100 --> 00:47:24.720
is it's ready for
your day to day work.

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:27.080
Like if I were working
in my previous job,

00:47:27.080 --> 00:47:31.160
I would be using this, you
know, to finish projects and do

00:47:31.160 --> 00:47:32.627
things on time.

00:47:32.627 --> 00:47:34.460
You know, it's at that
point where we really

00:47:34.460 --> 00:47:37.130
feel confident that you can use
it and depend on it in your day

00:47:37.130 --> 00:47:37.873
to day work.

00:47:37.873 --> 00:47:39.290
Now that doesn't
mean things like,

00:47:39.290 --> 00:47:40.940
yeah, the unit test
pass, we're good,

00:47:40.940 --> 00:47:44.000
or it passes R command
check or something like that.

00:47:44.000 --> 00:47:45.330
We want to build a good system.

00:47:45.330 --> 00:47:46.350
We want you to be
able to use it.

00:47:46.350 --> 00:47:47.990
We want to make sure
it works, but we also

00:47:47.990 --> 00:47:50.157
want you to learn it if
you've never seen it before.

00:47:50.157 --> 00:47:52.710
We don't just want to be like,
you know, read the man page.

00:47:52.710 --> 00:47:55.370
So at the very beginning of
the pandemic, what we did

00:47:55.370 --> 00:47:57.290
was spent a lot of
time, really writing

00:47:57.290 --> 00:47:58.800
a lot of good documentation.

00:47:58.800 --> 00:48:00.650
So tinymodels.org is a website

00:48:00.650 --> 00:48:02.977
that has some really
good content on it.

00:48:02.977 --> 00:48:05.310
You can see there's a section
there for getting started.

00:48:05.310 --> 00:48:06.810
There's a great
sequence of articles

00:48:06.810 --> 00:48:09.650
that will introduce you to
tinymodels, show you how to use it.

00:48:09.650 --> 00:48:11.510
There's a lot of
articles about if you

00:48:11.510 --> 00:48:13.970
want to have an example
of Bayesian optimization

00:48:13.970 --> 00:48:16.862
or bootstrapping to compute
confidence intervals

00:48:16.862 --> 00:48:19.320
and things like that, that's
all on tidymodels.org.

00:48:19.320 --> 00:48:20.940
There's quite a lot
of content there.

00:48:20.940 --> 00:48:23.768
In addition to that, you may
have seen the other night

00:48:23.768 --> 00:48:26.060
that Julia and I finished
writing a book on tidymodels

00:48:26.060 --> 00:48:27.590
from O'Reilly.

00:48:27.590 --> 00:48:29.070
They should be
printing them now.

00:48:29.070 --> 00:48:31.880
Hopefully we'll get copies
in the next week or two.

00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:33.410
The website's down
there, if you want

00:48:33.410 --> 00:48:35.540
to look at the bookdown version.

00:48:35.540 --> 00:48:37.320
So, so check that out.

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:40.520
It's a really good way, if you
like, longer form documentation

00:48:40.520 --> 00:48:41.670
to learn about tidymodels.

00:48:41.670 --> 00:48:44.180
So we think it's not
only ready for you,

00:48:44.180 --> 00:48:47.030
but we think we have lots and
lots of good materials publicly

00:48:47.030 --> 00:48:51.050
available for you to learn
it and interact with it.

