00:50:58.600 --> 00:51:00.850
So first question,
are you planning

00:51:00.850 --> 00:51:02.830
to integrate tidymodels
with other data

00:51:02.830 --> 00:51:05.770
structures like time series
or arrays for images, text

00:51:05.770 --> 00:51:06.760
or video?

00:51:06.760 --> 00:51:08.470
I'll answer first
and then you can go.

00:51:08.470 --> 00:51:13.300
So one of the things that is
especially applicable in text

00:51:13.300 --> 00:51:18.440
data is the a characteristic of
it is that it is really sparse.

00:51:18.440 --> 00:51:22.060
And so one thing that we 
did, have already integrated

00:51:22.060 --> 00:51:26.380
is the ability to
internally within that loop

00:51:26.380 --> 00:51:29.920
that we talked about past
data as a sparse matrix

00:51:29.920 --> 00:51:33.940
to models that can
handle it and thus

00:51:33.940 --> 00:51:36.350
train tune more effectively.

00:51:36.350 --> 00:51:39.250
So this is something that we
definitely think about and are

00:51:39.250 --> 00:51:40.550
interested in doing more of.

00:51:40.550 --> 00:51:43.700
Do you want to add more
on survival analysis or.

00:51:43.700 --> 00:51:45.543
Yeah, so for time
series and we've

00:51:45.543 --> 00:51:46.960
mentioned Matt
Dancho's work, he's

00:51:46.960 --> 00:51:49.763
here and has like a whole bunch
of packages that handle that.

00:51:49.763 --> 00:51:51.430
You know, a lot of
times what we work on

00:51:51.430 --> 00:51:55.828
is mostly about governed by what
we think, what we know about.

00:51:55.828 --> 00:51:57.370
And honestly, I
don't think any of us

00:51:57.370 --> 00:51:58.910
are expert at time series.

00:51:58.910 --> 00:52:01.270
So Matt's stuff covers all of
that I think really, really

00:52:01.270 --> 00:52:04.570
well we're doing more
with survival data

00:52:04.570 --> 00:52:07.060
we'll be more widely integrated
with like tune and things

00:52:07.060 --> 00:52:08.020
like that.

00:52:08.020 --> 00:52:09.862
Hopefully by the end
of the year, we're

00:52:09.862 --> 00:52:11.320
always on the look out, especially

00:52:11.320 --> 00:52:13.480
for recipes in terms
of specialized things

00:52:13.480 --> 00:52:14.420
that we can do.

00:52:14.420 --> 00:52:15.910
Like, I'm not
making any promises,

00:52:15.910 --> 00:52:18.160
but I used to work with a
lot of spectrometry data

00:52:18.160 --> 00:52:19.415
and I don't do that anymore.

00:52:19.415 --> 00:52:21.290
But in my mind, like,
that's the kind of data

00:52:21.290 --> 00:52:24.730
would be great for a recipes
package, like specialized

00:52:24.730 --> 00:52:26.620
recipes for that type
of data because they

00:52:26.620 --> 00:52:28.750
have somewhat unique
data structures

00:52:28.750 --> 00:52:29.810
and things they do to it.

00:52:29.810 --> 00:52:32.680
So I think as time
goes on, we want

00:52:32.680 --> 00:52:36.970
to find packages that satisfy
more niche, not markets,

00:52:36.970 --> 00:52:38.650
but you know, niche
set of users that

00:52:38.650 --> 00:52:42.160
would be really our
framework would be

00:52:42.160 --> 00:52:46.010
do a really good
job of capturing.

00:52:46.010 --> 00:52:49.730
Julia, could you comment on
how Vetiver compares and contrasts

00:52:49.730 --> 00:52:51.050
with MLflow?

00:52:51.050 --> 00:52:55.580
Sure I would say that
MLflow more highly

00:52:55.580 --> 00:53:03.500
prioritizes model development in
having opinionated requirements

00:53:03.500 --> 00:53:05.930
on how you develop your model.

00:53:05.930 --> 00:53:09.050
It then does some
similar things in that

00:53:09.050 --> 00:53:12.540
it provides a way for
you to deploy your model.

00:53:12.540 --> 00:53:14.540
One thing that is
different about Vetiver

00:53:14.540 --> 00:53:18.620
is that it is more flexible
in how you train your model,

00:53:18.620 --> 00:53:19.980
how you tune your model.

00:53:19.980 --> 00:53:22.910
If you've come from a more
statistical background,

00:53:22.910 --> 00:53:26.390
you're going to be more
comfortable with using

00:53:26.390 --> 00:53:31.040
your solid statistical
approaches to training

00:53:31.040 --> 00:53:31.860
your model.

00:53:31.860 --> 00:53:35.090
And then what Vetiver does
is it comes in at that point

00:53:35.090 --> 00:53:36.620
once your model is trained.

00:53:36.620 --> 00:53:39.200
So I highlight that
as a main difference

00:53:39.200 --> 00:53:44.840
between the approach of MLflow
and the approach of Vetiver.

00:53:44.840 --> 00:53:49.650
Max, how would a newcomer
get started with tidymodels?

00:53:49.650 --> 00:53:52.070
Or do you think
this is something

00:53:52.070 --> 00:53:55.910
that's only suitable for
seasoned data scientists?

00:53:55.910 --> 00:53:59.670
No, it's definitely, I
think, relevant for anybody.

00:53:59.670 --> 00:54:01.640
You know, it's a
little bit more verbose

00:54:01.640 --> 00:54:05.000
than things like caret, you
might end up writing a few more

00:54:05.000 --> 00:54:06.210
lines of code.

00:54:06.210 --> 00:54:09.140
But its simplicity in
a way, is a lot more

00:54:09.140 --> 00:54:11.540
it's a lot lower than,
let's say, caret,

00:54:11.540 --> 00:54:15.530
because you don't have for
the one thing you want to do.

00:54:15.530 --> 00:54:17.630
You don't have 75
options to sort through.

00:54:17.630 --> 00:54:20.535
So I think it's really
good for newcomers,

00:54:20.535 --> 00:54:22.160
especially if you
don't have, you know,

00:54:22.160 --> 00:54:24.348
we've shown UMAP and
racing and things like that.

00:54:24.348 --> 00:54:26.390
We don't want to make it
seem like you can't just

00:54:26.390 --> 00:54:28.397
fit your LM model to
whatever equivalent

00:54:28.397 --> 00:54:30.230
we're like mtcars
or something you have

00:54:30.230 --> 00:54:32.010
that's like not
terribly complex.

00:54:32.010 --> 00:54:33.720
So you can use it for that.

00:54:33.720 --> 00:54:34.880
You can use it.

00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:37.190
It's not necessarily
like optimized

00:54:37.190 --> 00:54:39.597
for inferential models,
but we have a lot of things

00:54:39.597 --> 00:54:40.680
that you can use for that.

00:54:40.680 --> 00:54:42.222
For example, like
multi-level models,

00:54:42.222 --> 00:54:44.760
like hierarchical Bayesian
models and mixed models.

00:54:44.760 --> 00:54:46.520
We have infrastructure for that.

00:54:46.520 --> 00:54:49.610
It's, you know, the scope of
it is a lot wider than maybe

00:54:49.610 --> 00:54:51.060
what we demonstrated here.

00:54:51.060 --> 00:54:52.730
But yeah, it's
pretty much usable

00:54:52.730 --> 00:54:54.890
for like simple
things for newcomers

00:54:54.890 --> 00:54:57.780
and, and then it lets you do
more and more as you go on.

00:54:57.780 --> 00:54:59.840
So definitely if you're
new, I'd start off

00:54:59.840 --> 00:55:03.290
with the Get Started part
of tidymodels.org

00:55:03.290 --> 00:55:08.440
and that'll probably do a good
job of leveling you up there.

00:55:08.440 --> 00:55:09.200
For both of you.

